⚙️ Core Modules
1. 🚪 Think First Gate
User cannot receive AI engagement until they type their own reasoning
Kills passive consumption habit immediately
2. 🗺️ Live Reasoning Map
Extracts: Assumptions, Emotions, Known Facts, Unknown Gaps
Visualised as a node diagram (vis.js or Mermaid)
User sees their own thinking for the first time
3. 🔍 Blind Spot & Bias Detector
Identifies cognitive biases in the user's reasoning
Every flag is transparent: what the bias is + the research paper behind it
Examples:
Confirmation Bias → Nickerson, R.S. (1998)
Automation Bias → Parasuraman & Manzey (2010)
Availability Heuristic → Kahneman (2011)
4. 🔗 Anti-Hallucination Transparency Layer
Any factual claim AI makes is tagged:
✅ Source cited
🟡 Confidence: High / Medium / Uncertain
⚠️ "This is inferred — please verify in your context"
AI never states something as fact without a source
5. 📊 Thinking Clarity Score
Measures per session:
Depth of reasoning before AI engagement
Number of blind spots caught
Whether the user changed their view based on their own reflection not AI output
Score improves over time = skill sharpening visible
6. 📉 AI Dependency Tracker
Tracks how often user reaches for AI vs thinks alone
If overuse detected: "You've used MindMirror 6 times today. Try this next one alone — your brain needs reps too."
Directly addresses gradual loss of human autonomy
7. 🔁 Spaced Repetition Learning Cycle
Problems you needed help with are resurfaced in 24hrs, 3 days, 1 week
"Last time you struggled with evaluating risk. Try it now — no AI."
Based on Ebbinghaus Forgetting Curve (1885)
Keeps skills sharp, not dependent
8. 🔒 Privacy by Design
Reasoning maps are local only — never stored on servers, never used for training
User owns their cognitive data entirely
👤 Target Users
User	Why They Need This
University students (18–25)	Using AI for assignments, losing ability to reason independently
Early-career professionals (22–30)	Making career/work decisions with AI, losing judgment confidence
Clinicians & lawyers	High-stakes decisions where cognitive surrender is dangerous
Educators	Want to teach critical thinking alongside AI literacy
📚 Psychology & Research Foundation
Research	Supports Which Feature
Kahneman, D. (2011). Thinking Fast and Slow	System 1 vs System 2 — why humans surrender to fast AI answers
Parasuraman & Manzey (2010). Complacency and bias in human use of automation. Human Factors	Cognitive surrender & automation bias detection
Flavell, J.H. (1979). Metacognition and cognitive monitoring. American Psychologist	Reasoning map + reflection loop design
Ebbinghaus, H. (1885). Memory: A Contribution to Experimental Psychology	Spaced repetition learning cycle
Nickerson, R.S. (1998). Confirmation bias: A ubiquitous phenomenon. Review of General Psychology	Blind spot detection layer
✅ Responsible AI Scorecard
Factor	How MindMirror Addresses It
Transparency	Every AI flag shows the psychology paper behind it
Anti-Hallucination	Source + confidence tag on every factual claim
Fairness	No personalization bias — same Socratic method for all users
Privacy	Reasoning maps local only, never stored or trained on
Human Autonomy	AI never decides — it challenges, then steps back
Trustworthy by Design	The entire product is built to make the human more reliable, not the AI
